<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<jmeterTestPlan version="1.2" properties="5.0" jmeter="5.6.3">
  <hashTree>
    <TestPlan guiclass="TestPlanGui" testclass="TestPlan" testname="bng perf">
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">The whole BNG performance suite as ONE plan, so a run produces ONE report — which
is what the CDP portal serves (it renders a single dashboard from the root of the
results prefix). Nothing needs selecting: an unset TEST_SCENARIO runs all of this.

Two thread groups answer "is everyday use healthy?", and seven answer "what
happens when uploads get big or numerous?".

EVERYDAY (runs first, on its own, so its numbers are uncontended)

  1. "Home page" — GET / on the public FRONTEND (no auth). A smoke check that the
     home page loads and returns 200 within maxResponseMs.

  2. "Project list endpoints" — the authenticated BACKEND list endpoints, covering
     BMD-933 "Only load the project details the list page actually needs". Drives
     GET /users/{userId}/projects and GET /projects under concurrency and asserts:
       AC1 Size Assertion   — list payload under listSizeLimitBytes (flat vs baseline size)
       AC2 Response Assertion — payload excludes the document-body keys habitats/postIntervention
       AC3 Response Assertion — payload includes the projected has_baseline flag
       AC4 Response Assertion — both endpoints accept limit/offset and return 200
       Duration Assertion   — guards the multi-second stall a multi-MB JSON.stringify causes
     This group is RED by design against an unfixed backend and PASSES once the
     projection + limit/offset fix lands. It REQUIRES a seeded environment (a project
     with a baseline visible to the token&apos;s user) — entrypoint.sh seeds via the API.

  Both are loop-count driven but capped by everydayPhaseDurationSeconds, so a slow
  backend cannot let them bleed into the upload phases behind them.

UPLOAD LOAD, which answers three questions a PM asks in this order:

  3. What does an everyday upload cost?       -&gt; "Size ramp", `everyday` label
  4. When does a BIG file become a problem?   -&gt; "Size ramp", the larger labels

     The ramp is ONE user doing a fixed, WEIGHTED pass: 20 everyday, 8 busy, 3 large,
     2 xlarge (sizeLoops*). One user is the point — these numbers only mean
     "the cost of validating this file" if nothing else is hitting the service, which
     rules out giving each size its own thread. But an unweighted pass makes every size
     share a sample count with the slowest one, so `everyday` — the number asked for
     first, and the only size real files actually reach — got as few samples as a 9 MB
     file did. The weights are roughly inverse to file size, so each size takes a
     comparable share of the window and the small ones earn a percentile rather than a
     single point. It is loop-count driven, so the counts are exact rather than
     "whatever fitted"; sizeRampDurationSeconds is a guard against a slow backend, and
     the window the schedule reserves for the phase.
  5. When do MANY big files become a problem? -&gt; "Concurrency ramp"
  6. What does the WHOLE journey cost end to end? -&gt; "Upload journey"

     The staircases above replay a PRE-STAGED upload, so they measure the
     service&apos;s validate cost in isolation. The journey groups instead drive a
     real upload per iteration — initiate, multipart POST to the CDP Uploader,
     validate — at 1/2/5 concurrent users, so the uploader and its virus scan
     are inside the measurement. The backend&apos;s validate route waits for the
     scan itself, so no client-side polling is needed; the &quot;validate+scan&quot; leg
     carries that wait, as a frontend user would experience it.

and, running alongside all of it, one that matters more than any of them:

  6. What does an ORDINARY user experience while that is happening?
     -&gt; "Everyday user (background probe)"

The probe is the point of the upload half. Upload latency getting worse under load
is expected; an unrelated project list going from 200ms to a timeout is an
availability story, and only a probe running concurrently shows it. The probe
starts AFTER the everyday groups finish, so the quiet stretch before the size ramp
is a genuine baseline to compare the loaded phases against.

Shape of the run. Every group is scheduled, so the phases run in sequence while
the probe spans the upload half. Nominal run is 495s:

              seconds 0  25  55                  215  255 290  325 360   395 430 465 495
  home + list         |=|
  probe                   |=============================================================|
  size ramp                  |==================|
  1 user                                          |==|
  2 users                                             |==|
  5 users                                                 |==|
  10 users                                                     |==|
  20 users                                                         |==|
  journey 1/2/5                                                          |==| |==| |==|

The stretch between the probe starting and the size ramp (30-55s here) is quiet
by construction: it is the baseline every loaded phase is read against.

The size ramp&apos;s 160s is not a round number, and is not a budget the ramp spends:
it is what its weighted pass NEEDS. The ramp is the one loop-count-driven phase,
so its window has to fit 20+8+3+2 validates or the scheduler cuts the pass off
mid-way — always at the tail, because it runs smallest first, so the sizes lost
are exactly the ones the ramp exists to measure. entrypoint.sh derives the window
from the weights and a per-size time allowance; change a weight there and this
default moves with it. summarise-run.mjs reports got-vs-expected per size, so a
cut-off pass is stated rather than left looking like an absent row.

Uploads are staged BEFORE JMeter starts (scripts/stage-uploads.mjs): initiate,
POST to the CDP Uploader, wait for the scan. Only the validate call is measured
- the rest is the uploader&apos;s work, not ours. The staging step also builds a pool
of projects, because validation only runs the full pipeline (extract, size,
persist) when a projectId is supplied, and concurrent uploads to the SAME
project serialise on a row lock and 409.

Assertions do not gate the task. A red Duration Assertion beyond N users IS the
result, not a failure - the report, not the exit code, is the source of truth.
The "valid":true assertion is different: if that goes red the staged fixture is
not passing validation and every number in the run is meaningless.

Runtime properties (all overridable with -J..., wired from env vars in entrypoint.sh):
  frontendDomain / backendDomain   per-service hosts (default *.${env}.cdp-int.defra.cloud)
  port / protocol / env            shared target port/scheme/environment
  bearerToken                      Bearer token. No default — entrypoint.sh mints a
                                   real cdp-defra-id-stub token.
  userId                           path segment for /users/{userId}/projects (the handler
                                   trusts the token sub, not this value). Default: me
  maxResponseMs                    home-page per-request time budget (default 2000)
  listSizeLimitBytes               max backend list response size (default 262144 = 256 KB)
  listMaxLatencyMs                 max backend list response time (default 2000)
  limit / offset                   pagination params exercised on both endpoints (default 50 / 0)
  homeThreads / homeRampSeconds / homeLoops   home-page load profile (default 1 / 1 / 5)
  listThreads / listRampSeconds / listLoops   backend load profile (default 10 / 10 / 20)
  everydayPhaseDurationSeconds     cap on the two everyday groups (default 25)
  uploadId_&lt;label&gt;                 staged upload ids, one per size label. A size that
                                   did not stage has none, and entrypoint.sh zeroes that
                                   phase&apos;s loop count rather than let it POST to
                                   /baseline/validate/ with an empty path segment.
  projectsCsv                      path to the staged project id pool
  probeDelaySeconds                when the probe starts (default 30)
  probeDurationSeconds             how long the background probe runs (default 465)
  probeThinkMs / probeMaxLatencyMs probe pacing and its latency budget
  validateBudgetMs                 latency budget for a validate call (default 30000)
  sizeRamp* / conc*                per-phase delay, duration and thread counts
  sizeRampThreads                  users on the size ramp (default 1). Set 0 — as with
                                   probeThreads / concUsers* — to suppress the phase, which
                                   is how a local run skips the upload half.
  sizeRampLoops                    weighted passes over the size ramp (default 1)
  sizeLoops&lt;Size&gt;                  samples per size in a pass (default 20/8/3/2). The
                                   ramp window must fit them — entrypoint.sh derives it.
  journeyUsers1/2/5 / journeyDelay1/2/5 / journeyStepDurationSeconds
                                   the upload-journey staircase (defaults 1/2/5 users,
                                   30s per step, scheduled after the concurrency ramp).
                                   Set a journeyUsers* to 0 to suppress that step.
  journeyFile                      the file each journey iteration uploads (default the
                                   committed everyday fixture)
  journeyBudgetMs                  latency budget for the validate+scan leg (default 20000)
  uploaderUrl                      the CDP Uploader base URL — the backend returns only
                                   the upload PATH, so the plan needs the host too
  uploadS3Bucket                   destination bucket for /upload/initiate. MUST be a
                                   bucket the environment&apos;s cdp-uploader may write to
                                   (its CONSUMER_BUCKETS); default is the local stack&apos;s
                                   baseline-files

Phase delays are ABSOLUTE seconds from the start of the run, so every duration change
has to be propagated to every later phase. Do NOT do that by hand: entrypoint.sh
DERIVES all of them from the phase durations plus PHASE_GAP_SECONDS, and the defaults
above are simply that derivation written out, so the plan is still self-consistent
when JMeter is driven directly. Override a duration via the env
vars and the delays move with it; override a delay and you own the arithmetic.</stringProp>
